  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nFog is the main forecast concern again this morning. Light NNE flow \noff Lake Huron supporting widespread dense fog over parts of SE MI \nand continuing to expand SW with time. Models split on exactly how \nfar SW this fog gets but there is a decent chance our NE counties \ncould get clipped. A few HRRR and RAP runs have also indicated \nadditional fog development possible around KFWA and points SE. Low \nforecast confidence precludes any proactive headline decisions for \nnow but will continue to monitor satellite/surface obs closely \nthrough the early morning and issue advisories or SPS's as \nnecessary. Otherwise, incredibly high amplitude/short wavelength \nridge continues to build over the Great Lakes through the weekend \nwith strong AVA/subsidence maintaining high pressure across the \nLower Great Lakes/northeast CONUS. Surface winds slowly veer to a \nmore SE and eventually S/SW direction tonight through the weekend. \nThat coupled with some increasing high clouds from upstream \ntrough/front should limit the fog threat for Sat AM though some \npatchy fog can't be ruled out for our eastern zones. High temps \nlikely reach the low 70s again today given sunny skies (outside of \nmorning fog/stratus) and deep mixing. Leaned on the warmer and drier \nside of guidance for today which gives afternoon RH values around 35 \npercent. This is not low enough for a substantive fire wx concern \n(especially given very light wind) but imagine finer fuels are \nstarting to get pretty dry. Similar story over the weekend. Sunday's \nhighs will be a little warmer given increasing SW flow/light WAA but \ndewpoints also creep up a bit as well. Strong ridge holds firm over \nthe Ohio Valley this weekend as upstream surface front stalls and \nwashes out well to our NW. Our next chance of rain doesn't arrive \nuntil next Thu and even that is highly questionable as numerous \nsolutions show this front stalling to our NW as well. Real rainfall \nchances don't arrive until around Halloween but that is far enough \nout that many things could still change. Main story is that warm, \ndry and pleasant fall weather will continue well into next week.",
